WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:05.520
Saved by the Bell is a beloved touchstone&nbsp;
for '90s kids everywhere. But for all its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:10.880
wholesome hijinks, the show's set was more than&nbsp;
a little sordid. These are the hook-ups, parties,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:14.960
and weirdest fashion choices that went on&nbsp;
behind the scenes of Saved by the Bell.

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:19.040
You may be surprised to learn that&nbsp;
Bell started off as a completely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:23.920
different show called Good Morning, Miss&nbsp;
Bliss. The pilot episode aired in 1988,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:27.680
starring Hayley Mills, a British&nbsp;
actress, as the titular Miss Bliss.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:32.480
The show lasted for 13 episodes on the&nbsp;
Disney Channel before it got canceled.

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:37.040
When Miss Bliss didn't make it in the ratings,&nbsp;
executive producer Peter Engel went back to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:42.400
drawing board. The series was retooled into Saved&nbsp;
By the Bell, taking four cast members with it:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:47.520
Mark-Paul Gosselaar as Zachary "Zack" Morris,&nbsp;
Dustin Diamond as Samuel "Screech" Powers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:52.960
Lark Voorhies as Lisa Turtle, and Dennis&nbsp;
Haskins as Mr. Belding. Brian Austin Green&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:58.080
and Jaleel White also appeared in a scrapped&nbsp;
pilot of Miss Bliss, but did not make the jump.

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:01.520
Though Miss Bliss takes place in&nbsp;
Indianapolis, Engel decided to move&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:05.920
the kids to California. He also changed&nbsp;
the age range of the kids on the show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:08.320
from eighth graders to full blown high-schoolers.

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:14.080
Saved by the Bell's beloved Zack Morris was&nbsp;
inspired by another high school smart aleck:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:18.160
Ferris Bueller. As Gosselaar detailed&nbsp;
to Jimmy Fallon, he snagged quite a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:22.800
of swagger from that cinematic slacker.&nbsp;
Morris' infamous moments of breaking the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:27.680
fourth wall to speak to the audience, for&nbsp;
example, are directly inspired by Bueller.

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:30.640
"That was done by Ferris Bueller,&nbsp;
we were just copying him."

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:34.480
Similarly, the character of A.C.&nbsp;
Slater was inspired by Vinny Barbarino,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:38.640
the character John Travolta made famous&nbsp;
on the '70's sitcom Welcome Back, Kotter.

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:44.400
"The first thing you gotta do is the&nbsp;
Vinny Barbarino look. Okay? Your hair&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:49.360
for instance. Very casual. It should look&nbsp;
like it's being blown by unseen winds."

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:50.400
Engel recalled:

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:53.120
"Slater was supposed to be&nbsp;
Vinny with a leather jacket.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:56.400
Italian kid, who was a street kid, an army brat."

00:01:56.400 --> 00:02:01.200
Mr. Belding was also inspired by someone&nbsp;
else, but not in a way the man in question&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:04.800
might have liked: The character&nbsp;
was named after Richard Belding,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:08.240
a pain-in-the-butt editor Engel&nbsp;
had to work with at Universal.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:13.360
Lisa Turtle's name came from a real girl Engel&nbsp;
knew, and Slater was named after a friend of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:18.800
Engel's son in kindergarten. By the way, the&nbsp;
A.C. in A.C. Slater stands for Albert Clifford.

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:23.440
While Gosselaar, Diamond, Haskins, and&nbsp;
Voorhies all made the move to Bell,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:29.280
Jennie Garth, who was up to play Kelly Kapowski,&nbsp;
didn't get the role. She went on to major success,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:35.520
however, on Beverly Hills 90210. Which might have&nbsp;
been for the best, since not everyone who did get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:40.720
cast in Saved by the Bell was thrilled about it:&nbsp;
Mario Lopez initially didn't want to play Slater,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:45.200
and admits in his autobiography he was&nbsp;
more interested in chasing girls than,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:49.840
quote, "trekking up to L.A. to read for a part&nbsp;
that had barely been sketched out on paper."

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:54.880
Engel reportedly fought to have Tiffani Amber&nbsp;
Thiessen on the show, knowing she was, quote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.880 --> 00:03:00.560
"going to be a major star." Elizabeth Berkley&nbsp;
was also up to play Kelly, but wound up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:05.520
playing Jessie, a much smarter, more evolved&nbsp;
character specifically created for Berkley.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:09.520
As she reflected on Bethenny, she&nbsp;
wasn't super into her wardrobe, saying:

00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:11.120
"Just because you are a feminist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:15.040
why can't you also dress in things that&nbsp;
make you feel girly and empowered?"

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:20.480
As you watch even the most wholesome family&nbsp;
sitcom, you just can't help but wonder:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:24.320
Who's hooking up with whom behind the&nbsp;
scenes? Unsurprisingly, putting a bunch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.320 --> 00:03:31.840
of teenagers together resulted in a whole&nbsp;
lot of romance on the Saved by the Bell set.

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:38.240
(couples kiss, audience "whoo")

00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:39.680
As Gosselaar told People:

00:03:39.680 --> 00:03:43.040
"All of us dated at one point&nbsp;
or another — it was incestuous!"

00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:46.080
While much of what went on behind the&nbsp;
scenes was innocent, some of it became&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:52.160
messy. As Lopez details in his memoir, he dated&nbsp;
Thiessen, but did not stay faithful, admitting:

00:03:52.160 --> 00:03:56.400
"I can't say that I was capable at that age&nbsp;
of following through on my noble intentions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.400 --> 00:04:00.560
There was so much beauty everywhere&nbsp;
and I had so much affection to share."

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:04.720
With Saved By the Bell being full of teens,&nbsp;
it's no surprise they did some partying.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:09.360
Theissen has admitted the first time she&nbsp;
sipped alcohol was with Gosselaar in Paris&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:14.000
during a press tour. Gosselaar told ABC News&nbsp;
he did the time-honored show biz tradition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:19.040
of using his celebrity to get into adult&nbsp;
clubs at the age of 16, although he insisted:

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:23.840
"But I didn't abuse it, and neither did my&nbsp;
castmates. I can't stress how good we were."

00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:27.280
Most of us probably have regrets&nbsp;
about our high school hair,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.280 --> 00:04:31.280
and the Saved By the Bell gang is&nbsp;
no exception. Many fans don't know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.280 --> 00:04:36.720
that Gosselaar is a natural brunette who had to&nbsp;
bleach his hair blond for every single episode.

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:38.640
“When they sent me the notes on you last night,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:41.824
it said you dyed your hair&nbsp;
blonde for every single episode.”

00:04:41.824 --> 00:04:46.400
"For six years! I'm surprised I have&nbsp;
hair! I can't believe I have hair now."

00:04:46.400 --> 00:04:49.920
Gosselaar's hair style looked different&nbsp;
every season because, surprisingly enough,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:53.440
the Bell gang thought every&nbsp;
season would be their last.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:57.120
The show ultimately lasted six&nbsp;
years, but as Gosselaar explained:

00:04:57.120 --> 00:05:00.080
"At the end of [every] season, we&nbsp;
all left, we said goodbye to each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.080 --> 00:05:04.160
other. And that's why every season my&nbsp;
hairstyle was so radically different."

00:05:04.160 --> 00:05:07.360
Hair drama spared no one. Not even AC Slater.

00:05:07.360 --> 00:05:10.080
"I didn't even know I had a mullet,&nbsp;
it was like an unintentional mullet."

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:13.840
Elizabeth Berkley had trouble keeping&nbsp;
her wild, curly hair under control,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:19.360
and was subjected to endless procedures,&nbsp;
treatments, and techniques. The actress recalled:

00:05:19.360 --> 00:05:21.760
"They diffused my hair, flipped it, teased it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.760 --> 00:05:25.680
sprayed it with hairspray. I practically&nbsp;
went through a can of hairspray a show."

00:05:26.240 --> 00:05:30.880
One of the most infamous and parodied Saved&nbsp;
By the Bell episodes is "Jessie's Song."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.880 --> 00:05:34.000
In it, Jessie gets hooked on&nbsp;
caffeine pills and finally has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:38.640
a breakdown in Zack's arms as she sings&nbsp;
the Pointer Sisters hit, "I'm So Excited."

00:05:39.200 --> 00:05:47.040
"I'm so excited, I'm so excited, I'm so… scared."

00:05:47.840 --> 00:05:53.440
This moment is widely derided as cheesy,&nbsp;
melodramatic, and unintentionally funny.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.440 --> 00:05:57.840
What few know is that this scene was&nbsp;
initially going to be a lot more serious.

00:05:57.840 --> 00:06:01.760
As Engel has detailed, Jessie was&nbsp;
originally going to be hooked on speed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.760 --> 00:06:04.640
but NBC's Standards and Practices stepped in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.640 --> 00:06:09.680
and told him to tone the episode down.&nbsp;
Still, the point came across. Engel said:

00:06:09.680 --> 00:06:13.840
"Today, when I meet fans of the show, 'Jessie's&nbsp;
Song' is almost always the episode that comes up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.840 --> 00:06:18.960
first. No one was making programming for kids&nbsp;
like that at the time. It made an impact. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:23.840
helped them grow up. And I'm still, to this&nbsp;
day, proud to have my name on that episode."

00:06:24.560 --> 00:06:29.680
Like so many child stars, the Bell cast got&nbsp;
screwed by the deal they signed. At the time, no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.680 --> 00:06:34.400
one suspected that Saved by the Bell would become&nbsp;
a huge phenomenon. Being young and naïve, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.400 --> 00:06:39.760
stars signed iffy contracts, meaning they didn't&nbsp;
make anywhere near as much money as you'd think.

00:06:39.760 --> 00:06:42.800
"We made really bad deals. Poor deals back then."

00:06:42.800 --> 00:06:46.160
"It is what it is. You move on, you learn."

00:06:46.160 --> 00:06:51.360
Moreover, the cast do not make money from the&nbsp;
show's syndication, nor from its merchandise.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.360 --> 00:06:55.200
Everyone makes mistakes when they're young,&nbsp;
but most of us don't have to live with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.200 --> 00:06:59.520
knowledge that those mistakes are still&nbsp;
costing us thousands of dollars every day.

00:07:00.320 --> 00:07:03.600
Dustin Diamond fell on hard&nbsp;
times after the show ended.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:07.680
The past few decades have been&nbsp;
littered with scandal and arrests.

00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:13.120
Most infamously, he wrote a salacious&nbsp;
tell-all book, 2009's Behind the Bell.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.120 --> 00:07:16.880
Its veracity is forcefully&nbsp;
disputed by his former castmates.

00:07:16.880 --> 00:07:19.360
"I'm guessing you have not&nbsp;
read that, or have you?"

00:07:19.360 --> 00:07:20.560
"I've read excerpts."

00:07:20.560 --> 00:07:22.400
"This guy's such a d---."

00:07:22.400 --> 00:07:27.280
To some degree, Diamond seems to agree with their&nbsp;
point of view. In recent years, he has claimed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.280 --> 00:07:32.720
that his ghostwriter, quote, "fabricated a whole&nbsp;
bunch of stuff," and that he was "railroaded."

00:07:32.720 --> 00:07:37.120
So that's a bummer. But you know what isn't?&nbsp;
Dennis Haskins, who played Mr. Belding,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.120 --> 00:07:41.120
finally graduated college&nbsp;
in 2015 at the age of 65,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.120 --> 00:07:45.280
earning a Bachelor of Arts in&nbsp;
theater and speech. For his part,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.280 --> 00:07:51.280
Haskins looks back fondly on his time as part&nbsp;
of a much-beloved TV classic, saying in 2013:

00:07:51.840 --> 00:07:55.680
"I knew it was something special because&nbsp;
the energy was different. The kids were a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.680 --> 00:08:00.240
little bit older, the dynamic was great between&nbsp;
everybody, and we were having a great time."

